# Exact approved release SHA (rule 229)

**Status:** ACTIVE from `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8D-APPROVED-SHA-GUARD-1` (2026-11-14, Program 8D PR2). Sprint
record: `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-8d-approved-sha-guard-1.md`. Supersedes rule 220 §13f and
`.claude/rules/deploy-infra.md` AIR-DEP-34 ("the runner deploys the tip of the base branch at launch").

## 1. The defect

`deploy-immutable-exec.sh` pinned `FETCH_HEAD` of the base branch. The pin was immutable once taken, but nothing tied
it to the commit the owner approved: a push between the approval and the fetch was deployed as if approved, and the
only protection was an operator running `git ls-remote` by hand.

## 2. The invariant

`DEPLOYED_SHA == PINNED_SHA == APPROVED_SHA`, where `APPROVED_SHA` is named by the operator **and** by a release
approval record merged on the base branch. If `RESOLVED_TARGET != APPROVED_TARGET` the answer is `DEPLOYMENT=DENIED`
(exit 77). Nothing is ever substituted: not the branch tip, the running HEAD, a tag or an earlier approval.

## 3. The approval record

`deploy/release-approvals/<release-id>.approval`, KEY=VALUE, parsed by `scripts/lib/release-approval.sh` and never
sourced or evaluated. Values are plain (`[A-Za-z0-9._:/+-]`); unknown, duplicate or missing keys are refused; at most
4096 bytes. Keys (contract 1): `DMS_APPROVAL_CONTRACT`, `_RELEASE_ID`, `_REPOSITORY`, `_HOST`, `_BASE_BRANCH`,
`_TARGET_SHA`, `_TARGET_TREE`, `_GO_TAG`, `_APPROVED_BY`, `_APPROVED_AT`, `_NOT_AFTER`, `_EVIDENCE`. The format and an
example are in `deploy/release-approvals/README.md`.

It is written only after the owner's explicit written approval of the exact merged commit (CG-22), committed together
with that approval in the evidence document it names, and merged through a pull request. Approval to implement or to
merge is never approval to deploy.

## 4. What the guard checks (all, or DENIED)

1. `--release-id` is a lowercase slug; `--approved-sha` is a full 40-character lowercase SHA (no abbreviation, ref,
   tag or `HEAD`).
2. This host is `DMS_PRODUCTION_HOSTNAME` and not a frozen host (`deploy/production-host.conf`).
3. `origin` of the checkout normalises to `DMS_PRODUCTION_REPOSITORY`; an `OWNER/REPO` authority accepts only a
   GitHub https/ssh URL (a local path spelling `OWNER/REPO` is refused).
4. `git fetch --no-tags origin +refs/heads/<branch>:refs/dms-release-approval/<pid>-<rand>` — a private per-run ref,
   removed on every path, never the shared `FETCH_HEAD` (another fetch on the host can overwrite that). The record
   is read from the fetched commit **object** (`git cat-file blob`), never from the working tree, an environment
   variable or another local file. Record and evidence must be regular-file tree entries (mode `100644`), not
   symlinks. No `safe.directory` override.
5. The record names this release, repository, host and branch; `TARGET_SHA` equals `--approved-sha`, is a commit
   (not a tree, blob or tag), and its tree equals `TARGET_TREE`; `APPROVED_BY` is in
   `DMS_PRODUCTION_RELEASE_APPROVERS`; `GO_TAG` ends `-go` and equals the approved commit's `.sprint/current.yml`.
6. Validity: canonical UTC instants, `APPROVED_AT <= now + 300 s`, `now <= NOT_AFTER`, window at most 72 hours.
7. `EVIDENCE` is a `docs/*.md` path present on the branch that quotes the full approved SHA.
8. The approved commit is an ancestor of the fetched tip, and every path changed between them (`git diff-tree`; a
   failed listing is a denial) is a release approval record or `docs/**/*.md`. Any other change means the approval
   no longer describes the branch: re-approve.
9. Forward only: the running HEAD and the local branch (which must exist) are ancestors of (or equal to) the
   approved commit. Going back is a rollback.

## 5. Where it runs

- `deploy-vps-runner.sh start|run --release-id <id> --approved-sha <sha>`: `approve_or_deny` runs **before** the run
  directory, log, record, links or the detached process exist. A refusal leaves nothing behind.
- `deploy-vps.sh`: refuses without both arguments before the hand-over; passes them to the bootstrap and to its own
  snapshot copy, which re-asserts `TARGET == APPROVED == DMS_DEPLOY_APPROVED_SHA` before the preflight, the secret
  hardening, the backup and the checkout, and again at the final HEAD check.
- `deploy-immutable-exec.sh --role deploy`: requires `--branch`, `--release-id`, `--approved-sha`; refuses
  `--target-sha` and `--snapshot-ref`; runs only `scripts/deploy-vps.sh` (a rollback only `scripts/rollback-vps.sh`);
  accepts no overlay but the live `deploy/runtime-identity.conf`;
  verifies the approval **inside the flock** and before the pin, the snapshot and any mutation; pins exactly the
  approved commit and prints `DEPLOY_APPROVED_SHA=` and `DEPLOY_APPROVAL_RECORD=`.
- The snapshot stages of `deploy-vps.sh` and `rollback-vps.sh` trust the `DMS_DEPLOY_*` values only inside the
  bootstrap's own snapshot: lock root `/run/daengtisiams-deploy` (or `/var/lock/…`), `<root>/<role>.lock`,
  `<root>/<role>-<run id>` owned by the deploy identity with mode 0700, and the lock **held** (`flock -n` fails).
- The guard and the authority it reads come from beside the executing program (the checkout, or the bundle of §7);
  the deploy program is the one beside the runner. There is no `DEPLOY_SCRIPT` or other environment override.
- `sprint-release.sh --apply` needs both arguments before the readiness check or its lock, passes them to `run`, and
  creates the GO tag on the approved commit only when the checkout is at it, never on `HEAD`; the tag name is the
  approved commit's own `go_tag` (`--go-tag` must equal it).
- `rollback-vps.sh`: the target must be an ancestor of the running checkout, checked before the hand-over and again
  inside the lock before the backup.

## 6. Evidence contract 3

The record (`deploy-<id>.host`) adds `release_id`, `approved_sha`, `approval_record` (`<tip>:<path>`) and
`approval_blob`. `verify <id>` accepts a success only when the contract-2 facts hold **and** the pinned target and the
checkout HEAD equal the record's `approved_sha`, the run's own log shows `RELEASE APPROVAL: GO`, exactly one
`DEPLOY_APPROVED_SHA=` equal to it and exactly one `DEPLOY_APPROVAL_RECORD=` for the same approval path, and both
approval blobs (record's and log's) exist as git objects, are the approval file at their recorded commit, that commit
carries the approved commit, and their `DMS_APPROVAL_RELEASE_ID` / `DMS_APPROVAL_TARGET_SHA` lines name this release
and commit. It then prints `DEPLOY_APPROVED_TARGET_MATCH=YES (<sha>)`. Contract-2 records are
`INCOMPATIBLE`. (The tips may differ: approvals or docs may land between the runner's check and the bootstrap's.)

## 7. Bootstrap (the first deploy that carries the guard)

A production tree older than this rule holds a runner without the guard, which must not launch the release that
installs it. The one-time procedure in `docs/runbooks/production-host-identity.md` §6c exports the approved commit's
`scripts/` and `deploy/` with `git archive <approved-sha>` into a new root-only directory, checks the runner's
SHA-256 against the commit object, and starts **that** runner with the same arguments. Every check above runs; nothing
in the live checkout is edited by hand; no transitional bypass exists. From then on the in-tree runner is used.

**In force on Biznet since 2026-11-14.** The bootstrap ran once there: release `dms-rme-revision-program-8d`, deploy
`20261114-011345`, approved commit `82f1eeba`, verified by the deployed in-tree runner (`DEPLOY_APPROVED_TARGET_MATCH=YES`,
`DEPLOY RUNNER OK`) and GO tagged `dms-rme-revision-program-8d-go` (`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-8d.md` §12).
The Biznet in-tree runner carries the guard, so §6c is spent there and every later deploy uses runbook §6b.

**CI cost of an approval record.** The gate classifier maps `deploy/release-approvals/*.approval` to no known
category, so an approval-record pull request fails closed to `unknown_high_risk` and runs the whole Critical Gate
(about an hour). That is correct; never reclassify the record as documentation to save time (CG-17). A dedicated
category is an open owner proposal (8D §12.7).

## 8. Trust model (what this does not prove)

The guard **binds** a deploy to a content-addressed record on the canonical base branch. It does not
**authenticate** a human, and nothing on the host can prove the record arrived through a reviewed pull request: the
base branch has no GitHub protection (checked 2026-11-12), so anyone able to push to it can write a record, and
`APPROVED_BY` is an attribution, not a signature. The governance is: record only after the owner's written approval
of the full SHA, through a pull request the owner merges (CG-22). Account separation is verifiable, human separation
is not (CG-07). Root on the production host is inside the trust boundary and can bypass any host-side check; the run
directory under `storage/logs` is writable by the runtime user (contract 3 cross-checks git objects, which it is
not). **Owner follow-ups (not blocking, recorded in the sprint doc):** branch protection or signed approval commits on
the base; a root-owned run directory; one lock shared by deploy and rollback.

## 9. Threats and where each is stopped

| Threat | Stopped by | Test (`DeployHardenApprovedShaGuardTest`) |
|---|---|---|
| Branch moved before fetch | §4.8 diff rule | `refuses when the branch moved past…` |
| Branch moved after the pin | pin = approved SHA, snapshot from it | `keeps the approved commit when the branch moves after the pin` |
| Missing / wrong / unauthorised approval | §4.4–4.5 | `refuses a release with no merged approval record`, `refuses a record whose facts do not match` |
| Expired, future or open-ended approval; replay | §4.6, §4.9 | `…facts do not match` (window cases), `never moves production backwards…` |
| Malformed SHA; tree/blob/tag object | §4.1, §4.5 | `refuses an approved SHA that is not a full…`, `…names a tree or blob…` |
| Unmerged commit | §4.8 ancestry | `refuses an approved commit that is not merged…` |
| Record injection / forgery in the working tree | parser; object read | `refuses a record it would have to execute…`, `…no merged approval record` |
| Detached runner launched unapproved | `approve_or_deny` first | `launches nothing for an unapproved start` |
| Wrong host / repository | §4.2–4.3 | `refuses the wrong host…`, `refuses a checkout whose origin…` |
| Forged or stale success evidence | contract 3 | `DeployHardenRunnerEvidenceContractTest` approval cases |
| Concurrent deploys | flock, unchanged | `serialises two approved runs…`, `DeployHardenConcurrencyLockTest` |
| GO tag on the wrong commit | §5 wrapper | `the release wrapper … tags the approved commit` |
| Rollback to an invalid ref | §5 ancestry | `a rollback refuses a target outside the deployed history…` |

